ing strip, the editor's Title was already empty because I had tapped Revert earlier. So strip cancel worked.\n\nBut what if the strip cancel stripped the WRONG photo (big.jpg)? That would explain big.jpg losing metadata at ~11:32 (the time of strip dialog). Actually the timestamp 11:32 aligns with when I did the Revert/Strip test on the Zulu copy! So maybe \"Strip All EXIF\" (or Revert) on the Zulu copy affected big.jpg? Hmm, but the Zulu copy is a different file.\n\nWait, but \"Revert\" on the Zulu copy: the Title field had ZZZ, I tapped Revert → field cleared. If Revert actually applied to the file (reverting metadata to original), it would clear EXIF on the Zulu copy only. Then I canceled the editor. Hmm.\n\nBut big.jpg's Modified 11:32 suggests big.jpg was written at 11:32. Did \"Revert\" write big.jpg? Only if the editor was bound to big.jpg, not the Zulu copy. But the editor title showed hughugeQ.jpg. Hmm.\n\nCould the app have a bug where the metadata editor writes to the previously edited photo? Or the \"Revert\" writes to a stale reference? That's possible but hard to prove.\n\nAlternatively, maybe big.jpg's \"Title T2\"/capture metadata was never actually written to the file, only to an in-memory cache/MediaStore, and the \"Modified 11:32\" is from some other operation (maybe the app rewrote it when displaying?). Hmm.\n\nThis is getting deep. But the point: metadata changes are unreliable — a real bug in metadata editing. However, the developer rejected my capture-persistence report. Maybe because the specific steps didn't match. Hmm.\n\nLet me check the metadata editor for big.jpg now: Title should be empty and Captured empty. If Title T2 is gone, that's metadata loss. Let me verify. Tap Edit metadata & EXIF."}
