element_ref 2, observed_quote \"1 of 1 · hughugeQ.jpg\". Expected: \"1 of 1 · WAIT\". Observed: title shows filename instead of the saved Title. Hmm, but that's the \"title display\" not persistence.\n\nMaybe file from the editor: open Edit metadata & EXIF, see Title empty. Element ref 9. observed_quote would be the hint \"Title\". The expected: Title field shows WAIT. This is the \"not_persisted\" symptom.\n\nLet me first confirm the editor is empty now. Tap Details → Edit metadata. Then file.\n\nActually, I worry the dev will reject again. Let me think about whether this is a real app bug or a test-environment artifact. The app says \"Android will ask before changing media owned by another app.\" Maybe on emulator, modifying media requires the MediaStore createWriteRequest permission; the app may be using it and the system auto-allows. If not, the write fails. The immediate display is optimistic. This could be a genuine bug the developers care about. But maybe it's expected behavior in the harness (the seed media is owned by another app/package, so edits require a dialog that doesn't appear). Hmm.\n\nWait, actually, the seed media may be owned by the gallery app itself? The path /storage/emulated/0/Pictures/IrisSeed/*.jpg and URL content://media/external/images/media/... The owner package might be the app that created them (maybe com.iris.gallery or the test setup). If another app (e.g., com.android.providers.media) owns them, the app needs permission. The note appears in the editor, suggesting the app is aware.\n\nHmm. The fact that \"Modified\" updates to the save time suggests the file was rewritten (so the write did happen). But then the title is lost. Perhaps the app rewrites the JPEG but fails to embed the title, or embeds it in XMP that its reader doesn't read. That's a real app bug (write without read consistency).\n\nActually, maybe the app writes the title to MediaStore's XMP document but the reader reads EXIF UserComment, etc. Hmm.\n\nOK, regardless, fr