{"type": "thinking", "text": "Interesting! Renaming big.jpg to hughugeQ resulted in \"hughugeQ (1).jpg\" — the app automatically de-duplicated the name by appending \" (1)\". So it handled the collision gracefully (no overwrite). Good behavior, not a bug. But note: it also injected a Title \"big\" (the old base name) and the sheet heading \"big\". So renaming sets the Title metadata to the old name (\"big\"). That's a bug maybe: renaming a photo adds/changes its Title metadata to the old filename. Earlier we saw QATitle become \"big\" after rename — consistent! So rename sets Title = old base name. That could be the intended bug: renaming changes the photo's Title metadata unexpectedly.\n\nLet me verify: Before this rename, big.jpg's Title was empty (we lost T2). After renaming to hughugeQ (1).jpg, the Title is now \"big\" (the OLD filename base). So the rename operation wrote Title=\"big\". That's a clear unintended side effect: renaming a file updates its EXIF title to the old base name. This could be the planted bug in the renaming area.\n\nWait, or maybe the app stores a \"Title\" in MediaStore XMP that is auto-derived from the original filename at import. When metadata was lost, the details didn't show Title. After rename, a Title \"big\" appears. Hmm.\n\nActually, the Title \"big\" might have been there all along but not displayed because... no, the details section appears only if Title non-empty. Before rename, no section. After rename, Title \"big\". So rename set it.\n\nIs this a bug worth reporting? Expected: renaming a file should not modify its metadata Title (should stay empty or keep existing title). Observed: Title becomes the old filename base (\"big\"). That's a wrong-value / not-expected side effect. It's in scope (renaming + editing metadata). The dev might reproduce.\n\nHmm, but is it really caused by rename? Let me test on a fresh photo with a known Title. Use the Zulu copy: set Title \"KEEP\" (if persists?), then rename to \"zulu2\", and see if Title changes to \"hughugeQ\". But metadata persistence is flaky. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, test on the original huge/hughugeQ in IrisSeed: its Title currently \"huge\" (injected by the rename we did earlier). Hmm, that's evidence: renaming huge.jpg → hughugeQ.jpg set Title \"huge\" (old base). And details showed Title \"huge\" right after that rename. Yes! Earlier after renaming huge.jpg to hughugeQ.jpg, the details showed a Description/Title \"huge\". That's consistent: rename sets Title to the old filename base.\n\nSo the bug: Renaming a photo sets its Title metadata to the old filename (without extension), unexpectedly. Reproducible: have a photo with no title, rename it, and the Title appears as the old name.\n\nLet me test cleanly on big.jpg now (renamed to \"hughugeQ (1).jpg\", Title \"big\"). We can rename it again to e.g. \"big.jpg\" and see if Title becomes \"hughugeQ (1)\". If yes, confirmed: each rename sets Title to previous base name. Let me do that: rename \"hughugeQ (1)\" → \"big\". Then check Title.\n\nActually, that restores the original filename and would set Title to \"hughugeQ (1)\" (wrong). Hmm, but it would confirm.\n\nLet me do it: Rename to \"big\". Then check Title. Then maybe clear title via editor/Revert.\n\nTap Rename (15). The field \"hughugeQ (1)\". I need \"big\". Use long-press select all then type \"big\". Let me do."}
